msgid ":class:`~felupe.CharacteristicCurvePlugin`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:48
msgid "Records a force-displacement curve of a boundary condition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:49
msgid "``felupe[plot]`` (for plotting)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:50
msgid ":class:`~felupe.AnimationWriterPlugin`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:51
msgid "Writes an animation (GIF or MP4) of the deformed items."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:52
msgid "``felupe[view,io]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:53
msgid ":class:`~felupe.DashboardWidgetPlugin`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:54
msgid ""
"Shows a live dashboard of the job in a Jupyter widget or in a live HTML "
"file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:55
msgid "``felupe[dashboard]`` (for the widget)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:56
msgid ":class:`~felupe.LinesearchPlugin`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:57
msgid ""
"Scales the Newton increment by a step length (backtracking line search)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:59
msgid ":class:`~felupe.CutbackPlugin`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:60
msgid "Subdivides failed substeps into smaller increments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:65
msgid ""
"The progress is shown by default. Its verbosity is controlled by the "
"argument ``verbose`` of :meth:`~felupe.Job.evaluate`: a progress bar for "
"``True`` (default), a text-based output of the Newton-Raphson iterations for"
" ``2`` and no output for ``False``. The progress bar requires ``tqdm``, "
"otherwise the text-based output is used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:77
msgid ""
"A XDMF time series result file is written if a filename is given. Additional"
" point- and cell-data may be passed by the arguments ``point_data`` and "
"``cell_data``. The result file may be opened in ParaView."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:87
msgid ""
"The displacement of the first point and the sum of the reaction forces of a "
":class:`~felupe.Boundary` are recorded after each completed substep. A "
":class:`~felupe.CharacteristicCurve` is a job with a built-in characteristic"
" curve plugin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:102
msgid ""
"A frame of the deformed items is written after each completed substep. "
"Additional keyword-arguments are passed to the plot-methods of the items, "
"e.g. ``solid.plot(name, off_screen=True)``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:118
msgid ""
"The dashboard is shown in the output of the cell which evaluates the job, "
"e.g. in JupyterLab or in the interactive window of VS Code. It shows the "
"deformed model of the latest converged substep, the load path including "
"cutbacks, the convergence of the Newton-Raphson method and performance "
"statistics. With a filename, a live HTML file is written, which updates "
"itself in the web browser. This also works for a script in a terminal "
"(without a Jupyter frontend)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:135
msgid ""
"The step length of the Newton increment is halved until the deformation "
"gradients are admissible and the residuals decrease sufficiently. The line "
"search is also available for :func:`~felupe.newtonraphson`. For a "
":class:`~felupe.SolidBodyNearlyIncompressible`, see the notes of "
":class:`~felupe.LinesearchPlugin`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:150
msgid ""
"If the Newton-Raphson method of a substep fails, the unknowns and the state "
"of the items are restored and the substep is subdivided into smaller "
"increments. Custom items with a state, which is modified during the Newton "
"iterations, have to provide the methods ``checkpoint()`` and "
"``restore(checkpoint)``, see :class:`~felupe.mechanics.Item`. The time of a "
":class:`~felupe.TimeStep` is interpolated, i.e. each increment of a time-"
"dependent material takes its own time increment, see :doc:`umat_time`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:167
msgid "Combine Plugins"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:169
msgid ""
"Plugins may be combined. The hooks of the plugins are called in the order of"
" the list, followed by the plugins of :meth:`~felupe.Job.evaluate` (the "
"progress and the result file). A failed line search leads to a cutback. The "
"increments of a subdivided substep are internal to the substep, i.e. plugins"
" which record or write results after a completed substep, like the "
"characteristic curve, are called only once per substep."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:188
msgid ""
"List a :class:`~felupe.DashboardWidgetPlugin` before a "
":class:`~felupe.CutbackPlugin` to show the error messages of failed "
"attempts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:192
msgid "Custom Plugins"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:194
msgid ""
"A custom plugin is a subclass of :class:`~felupe.Plugin`. All hooks are "
"optional, i.e. only the required hooks have to be implemented. Each hook "
"takes the context, e.g. the job, the step or the result of a substep, and "
"the current state, e.g. the step number or the norm of the residuals of an "
"iteration, see :class:`~felupe.Context`, :class:`~felupe.JobState`, "
":class:`~felupe.SubstepState` and :class:`~felupe.IterationState`. The hooks"
" are called in the following order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:217
msgid ""
"This plugin counts the Newton-Raphson iterations, including the iterations "
"of failed attempts of a :class:`~felupe.CutbackPlugin`. The hook "
"``after_job`` is also called if the evaluation of the job fails."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:241
msgid ""
"The hooks of the Newton-Raphson method are also called by "
":func:`~felupe.newtonraphson`, i.e. the same plugin may be passed to "
"``newtonraphson(items, plugins=[counter], **loadcase)``. The hooks of the "
"job, the steps and the substeps are only called by a job."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:246
msgid ""
"A callable, e.g. a function ``plugin(context, state)``, is a simple plugin "
"which is called after each completed substep."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:260
msgid ""
"Plugins may also change the evaluation. In the hook ``after_linear_solve``, "
"the update of the unknowns may be modified, see "
":class:`~felupe.IterationState`. In the hook ``after_failed_substep``, a "
"failed substep may be recovered, see :class:`~felupe.SubstepState`. The "
":class:`~felupe.LinesearchPlugin` and the :class:`~felupe.CutbackPlugin` are"
" implemented by these hooks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/plugins.rst:268
msgid ""
"Plugins replace the callbacks of a :class:`~felupe.Job` and of "
":func:`~felupe.newtonraphson` (removed in v12.0.0). The migration is shown "
"in the notes of :class:`~felupe.Job` and of :func:`~felupe.newtonraphson`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/project.rst:2
msgid "Project Cell Values to Mesh Points"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/project.rst:4
msgid ""
"This section demonstrates how to move cell-values, located at the quadrature"
" points of cells, to mesh-points. The results of :func:`~felupe.topoints`, "
":func:`~felupe.tools.extrapolate` and :func:`~felupe.project` are compared "
"for the Cauchy stresses of a rectangular block under compression."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/project.rst:22
msgid ""
"Cell-based results, like Cauchy stresses, are not projected to mesh-points "
"by default. The means of the cell-values are plotted if no projection method"
" is specified. Different methods may be used to *move* the cell-data to the "
"mesh-points."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/project.rst:26
msgid ""
"With :func:`~felupe.topoints`, the cell-values are translated to and "
"averaged at the mesh-points."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/project.rst:29
msgid ""
"With :func:`~felupe.tools.extrapolate`, the cell-values are extrapolated to "
"the mesh- points by evaluating the cell-values at the quadrature points of "
"the cells and extrapolating them to the mesh-points."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/project.rst:33
msgid ""
"With :func:`~felupe.project`, the cell-values are projected to the mesh-"
"points by solving a least-squares problem."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:2
msgid "Use Solid Bodies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:4
msgid ""
"The mechanics submodule contains classes for the generation of solid bodies."
" Solid body objects are supported as items of a :class:`~felupe.Step` and "
"the :func:`~felupe.newtonraphson` function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:9
msgid "Solid Body"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:11
msgid ""
"The generation of internal force vectors and stiffness matrices of solid "
"bodies are provided as assembly-methods of a :class:`~felupe.SolidBody` or a"
" :class:`~felupe.SolidBodyNearlyIncompressible`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:34
msgid ""
"During assembly, several results are stored, e.g. the gradient of the strain"
" energy density function per unit undeformed volume w.r.t. the deformation "
"gradient (first Piola-Kirchhoff stress tensor). Other results are the "
"deformation gradient or the fourth-order elasticity tensor associated to the"
" first Piola-Kirchhoff stress tensor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:39
msgid ""
"\\boldsymbol{F} &= \\frac{\n"
"    \\partial \\boldsymbol{x}}{\\partial \\boldsymbol{X}\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"\\boldsymbol{P} &= \\frac{\n"
"    \\partial \\psi(\\boldsymbol{C}(\\boldsymbol{F}))}{\\partial \\boldsymbol{F}\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"\\mathbb{A} &= \\frac{\n"
"    \\partial^2 \\psi(\\boldsymbol{C}(\\boldsymbol{F}))\n"
"}{\\partial \\boldsymbol{F}\\ \\partial \\boldsymbol{F}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:61
msgid ""
"The Cauchy stress tensor, as well as the gradient and the hessian of the "
"strain energy density function per unit undeformed volume are obtained by "
"evaluate-methods of the solid body."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:65
msgid ""
"\\boldsymbol{P} &= \\frac{\n"
"    \\partial \\psi(\\boldsymbol{C}(\\boldsymbol{F}))}{\\partial \\boldsymbol{F}\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"\\mathbb{A} &= \\frac{\n"
"    \\partial^2 \\psi(\\boldsymbol{C}(\\boldsymbol{F}))\n"
"}{\\partial \\boldsymbol{F}\\ \\partial \\boldsymbol{F}}\n"
"\n"
"\\boldsymbol{\\sigma} &= \\frac{1}{J} \\boldsymbol{P} \\boldsymbol{F}^T"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:87
msgid "Body Force (Gravity) on a Solid Body"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:89
msgid ""
"The generation of external force vectors or stiffness matrices of body "
"forces acting on solid bodies are provided as assembly-methods of a "
":class:`~felupe.SolidBodyForce`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:93
msgid "\\delta W_{ext} = \\int_V \\delta \\boldsymbol{u} \\cdot \\rho \\boldsymbol{g} \\ dV"
msgstr "\\delta W_{ext} = \\int_V \\delta \\boldsymbol{u} \\cdot \\rho \\boldsymbol{g} \\ dV"

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:107
msgid "Pressure Boundary on a Solid Body"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:109
msgid ""
"The generation of force vectors or stiffness matrices of pressure boundaries"
" on solid bodies are provided as assembly-methods of a "
":class:`~felupe.SolidBodyPressure`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:112
msgid ""
"\\delta W_{ext} = \\int_{\\partial V}\n"
"    \\delta \\boldsymbol{u} \\cdot (-p) \\ J \\boldsymbol{F}^{-T} \\ d\\boldsymbol{A}"
msgstr ""
"\\delta W_{ext} = \\int_{\\partial V}\n"
"    \\delta \\boldsymbol{u} \\cdot (-p) \\ J \\boldsymbol{F}^{-T} \\ d\\boldsymbol{A}"

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solid.rst:138
msgid ""
"For axisymmetric problems the boundary region has to be created with the "
"attribute ``ensure_3d=True``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solvers.rst:2
msgid "Use External Solvers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solvers.rst:4
msgid ""
"FElupe uses SuperLU as direct sparse solver by default because it is shipped"
" with SciPy (and SciPy is already a dependency of FElupe). The default "
"solver :class:`~felupe.solve.SuperLUSolver` uses options of "
":func:`~scipy.sparse.linalg.splu` which are tuned for the (structurally) "
"symmetric matrices of the finite element method, also with non-symmetric "
"values, e.g. of frictional contacts. While it is definitely a good choice "
"for small to mid-sized problems, faster alternatives are easy to install and"
" use. This section demonstrates several possibilities, e.g. a fast direct "
"solver from `PyPardiso <https://github.com/haasad/PyPardisoProject>`_ (``pip"
" install pypardiso``) and the :func:`~scipy.sparse.linalg.minres` iterative "
"solver from ``SciPy``. Custom solvers may be passed to the evaluation of a "
":class:`~felupe.Job`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solvers.rst:24
msgid "Solvers from SciPy Sparse:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solvers.rst:46
msgid "``minres`` may be replaced by another iterative method, e.g. ``cg``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solvers.rst:57
msgid "Solvers from external packages:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solvers.rst:61
#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/solvers.rst:73
msgid ""
"Ensure to have `PyPardiso <https://github.com/haasad/PyPardisoProject>`_  "
"installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat.rst:2
msgid "Define Strain-Based Materials"
msgstr ""

