msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:2
msgid "Define Time-Dependent Materials"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:4
msgid ""
"A time-dependent material, e.g. a viscoelastic material, takes the time "
"increment :math:`\\Delta t` as an input, like the deformation gradient. The "
"time increment is not a material parameter, it is given by the time of the "
"load path, e.g. by the ramp of a :class:`~felupe.Step`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:10
msgid "Time-Dependent Materials"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:12
msgid ""
"A material with a given function, i.e. :class:`~felupe.Hyperelastic`, "
":class:`~felupe.MaterialAD`, :class:`~felupe.Material`, "
":class:`~felupe.MaterialStrain` or the materials of the JAX backend, is "
"time-dependent if its function has an argument ``dtime`` without a default "
"value, which is not given as keyword-argument of the material. Then, the "
"attribute ``time_dependent`` is True and the time increment is given by the "
"keyword-argument ``dtime`` of the methods ``gradient()`` and ``hessian()``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:33
msgid ""
"The built-in time-dependent materials are "
":func:`~felupe.finite_strain_viscoelastic` for :class:`~felupe.Hyperelastic`"
" and :func:`~felupe.linear_elastic_viscoelastic` for "
":class:`~felupe.MaterialStrain`. Their initial state variables are zero. The"
" materials which wrap other materials, e.g. "
":class:`~felupe.NearlyIncompressible`, :class:`~felupe.ThreeFieldVariation` "
"or a composite material ``umat & other_umat``, pass the time increment to "
"their time-dependent materials."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:49
msgid ""
"A time increment which is given as keyword-argument of the material, e.g. "
"``Hyperelastic(finite_strain_viscoelastic, mu=1.0, eta=1.0, dtime=1.0, "
"nstatevars=6)``, is a constant material parameter. Then, the material is not"
" time-dependent. This is convenient for a load path with equal time "
"increments, e.g. a quick plot of a force-stretch curve. However, a constant "
"time increment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:55
msgid ""
"is the same for all substeps, i.e. there are no holding times or varying "
"rates,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:56
msgid ""
"is not scaled by a :class:`~felupe.CutbackPlugin`, i.e. each increment of a "
"subdivided substep takes the full time increment (with a warning) and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:58
msgid "is not optimized by :meth:`~felupe.ConstitutiveMaterial.optimize`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:61
msgid "Time Steps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:63
msgid ""
"A solid body, i.e. :class:`~felupe.SolidBody`, "
":class:`~felupe.SolidBodyNearlyIncompressible` or "
":class:`~felupe.TrussBody`, passes its time increment ``time_step`` to its "
"time-dependent material. The time increment is updated by a "
":class:`~felupe.TimeStep`. Its (absolute) time is ramped in a "
":class:`~felupe.Step`, like the boundary conditions. The time increment of a"
" substep is the difference to the time of the previous substep, the initial "
"time is zero. Hence, the first substep of a ramp which starts at zero has a "
"time increment of zero, which is handled by the time-dependent materials (no"
" evolution)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:105
msgid ""
"The same time step item is used in all steps, i.e. the time continues. The "
"position of the time step item in the list of items of a step doesn't "
"matter, because all ramped items are updated before the iterations of a "
"substep. If a substep is subdivided by a :class:`~felupe.CutbackPlugin`, the"
" time is interpolated like the other values of the ramp, i.e. each increment"
" of the substep takes its own time increment. The (absolute) time is written"
" as the time of a XDMF result file by "
"``job.evaluate(filename=\"result.xdmf\", time=time)``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:114
msgid "Force-Stretch Curves and Material Parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:116
msgid ""
"The force-stretch curves of a time-dependent material, e.g. by ``plot()``, "
"require the (absolute) times of the load cases. They are given as second row"
" of a load case, e.g. ``ux=[stretches, times]``. Each load case starts at "
"the initial time zero. A holding time is given by repeated stretches."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:128
msgid ""
"The material parameters of a time-dependent material are fitted to "
"experimental data with the times of the data points as third row, e.g. "
"``ux=[stretches, forces, times]``. The time increment is not a material "
"parameter, i.e. it is not fitted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:137
msgid "Custom Time-Dependent Materials"
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:139
msgid ""
"The function of a custom material takes the time increment as argument "
"``dtime`` (without a default value), e.g. a strain energy density function "
"of :class:`~felupe.Hyperelastic`. A time increment of zero has to be "
"supported, e.g. for the first substep of a ramp which starts at zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:154
msgid ""
"A subclass of :class:`~felupe.ConstitutiveMaterial` is time-dependent by the"
" class attribute ``time_dependent = True``. Its methods ``_gradient(x, "
"dtime)`` and ``_hessian(x, dtime)`` take the time increment, which is the "
"same for all chunks of cells."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:178
msgid ""
"Materials of other packages, which are not derived from "
":class:`~felupe.ConstitutiveMaterial`, are time-dependent if they have an "
"attribute ``time_dependent`` which is True. Then, their methods "
"``gradient(x, dtime)`` and ``hessian(x, dtime)`` take the time increment as "
"keyword-argument ``dtime``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../felupe/docs/howto/umat_time.rst:185
msgid ""
"The materials of the JAX backend are just-in-time compiled, i.e. the time "
"increment is a traced value. Instead of a Python condition, e.g. ``if dtime "
"> 0``, use :func:`jax.numpy.where`, see "
":class:`felupe.constitution.jax.Hyperelastic`. The time increment is passed "
"as an array of data type float, i.e. the compiled functions are re-used for "
"all time increments."
msgstr ""
